  "markdown_report": "**Synthesis.** Three starting insights guide this early-stage proposal. (1) Existing margins measure training discrimination. They cannot establish immediate-purpose relevance or validation generalization. (2) The backbone implementation is unavailable. Left-padding readout and causal isolation therefore require explicit checks. (3) Feedback favors specific recent connections. It motivates sensitivity tests, but does not establish that stronger recency improves HR@10.\n\nThree literature insights refine the approach. (1) NextItNet’s causal convolutions bound accessible history [1]. Receptive-field eligibility should therefore constrain comparisons between older and recent events. (2) Sampled metrics can reverse model comparisons [2]. Diagnostic rankings must remain separate from the pinned selection objective. (3) Recency dependence can become excessive [3]. Perturbations should measure dependence before motivating stronger recency weighting.\n\n**Directions and framework.** Organize evidence into three layers: implementation integrity → measured behavioral sensitivity → hypothesis generation. Integrity includes padding, causality, and receptive-field controls; sensitivity includes matched history edits and target-rank changes; hypothesis generation compares these measurements with qualitative feedback. Purpose, duplication, and compatibility remain unknown unless public titles/descriptions provide evidence. No unavailable ratings, categories, reviews, or simulator traits enter the framework.\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality / future potential / code difficulty, each 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n- **Contract-checked sensitivity adapter: 5/8/3.** Originality: combines architecture controls with behavioral probes, although its components are established. Potential: creates reusable evidence for subsequent experiments, although diagnostic improvement need not improve HR@10. Difficulty: requires inference hooks only, although receptive-field accounting needs architecture inspection [1,3].\n- **Matched recent-versus-older deletion probe: 5/7/4.** Originality: matching eligibility and edit size sharpens attribution, although deletion tests are established. Potential: tests stale-history dependence, although edits also shift positions. Difficulty: avoids retraining, although meaningful coherence matching needs reliable text evidence [1,3].\n- **Candidate-size stability audit: 3/6/3.** Originality: applies established sampling critiques, with little methodological novelty. Potential: reveals ranking fragility, although it cannot replace benchmark selection. Difficulty: reuses logits, although full-catalog scoring adds cost [2].\n\n**Selected idea: contract-checked sensitivity adapter.** At 0% progress, prioritize trustworthy measurement before architecture changes. Preserve one independently trained NextItNet checkpoint per category and all host interfaces. Validate short left-padded readout, future-token isolation, and architectural receptive-field boundaries. Then compare unchanged, prefix-shuffled-with-last-item-fixed, and matched recent/older deletion histories using identical candidates.\n\nReport paired target-rank changes, margin changes, hit@10 flips, eligible-user counts, and missing-data reasons. Use evaluation mode and seed 42; describe probes on optimized training prefixes as training diagnostics. Empty measurements return null with zero counts. Preserve `DiagnosisProbe(view, device)`, `run_full_diagnosis(train_loader)`, and the interpreter prompt grounded in metric definitions. Recommendations and model selection remain governed by the unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10.\n\nReferences: [1] [NextItNet](https://fajieyuan.github.io/papers/nextitnet6.pdf). [2] [Krichene–Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/). [3] [Measuring Recency Bias](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a contract-checked, inference-only diagnostic adapter for the single trained NextItNet model in each category. Establish implementation integrity before measuring dependence on recent versus older history.",
    "motivation": "Current probes measure training-position margins but do not verify sequence readout, causality, or temporal sensitivity. Qualitative feedback motivates investigating stale-history dependence without assuming observed interactions reveal current intent.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Access the actual network through view.model and preserve item_emb, log2feats(sequence), and predict_score(features, items). Inspect the supplied architecture before computing receptive-field bounds; skip unsupported controls with explicit reasons. For left-padded inputs, locate the maximum index whose item ID is nonzero; never use count minus one. Exclude empty histories. Build chronological training-prefix probes without including the target or later events in the input, and explicitly label their results as training diagnostics if those interactions were optimized. Use fixed unique candidate sets excluding the target from negatives, evaluation mode, seed 42, matched eligible perturbations, and explicit denominators. Return metrics and metric_definitions; undefined values are null. Never infer unavailable metadata or modify deployed scores. Any later architecture change must update both the predictor writer and diagnostic controls.",
    "pseudocode": "class DiagnosisProbe:\n    def __init__(self, view, device):\n        self.view, self.device = view, device\n\n    def run_full_diagnosis(self, train_loader):\n        # Preserve and restore the trained model's original mode.\n        # Disable dropout and gradients during every measurement.\n        controls = check_padding_causality_and_receptive_field(self.view)\n        records = []\n        for _, sequence, positive, negative in bounded_batches(train_loader):\n            for row in eligible_rows(sequence, positive):\n                t = max_index_where(sequence[row] != 0)\n                prefix = sequence[row, :t + 1]\n                target = positive[row, t]\n                candidates = fixed_unique_candidates(target, negative[row], seed=42)\n                variants = matched_eligible_history_edits(prefix)\n                for name, history in variants.items():\n                    x = left_pad(history)\n                    j = max_index_where(x != 0)\n                    features = self.view.log2feats(x)\n                    scores = self.view.predict_score(features[:, j, :], candidates)\n                    records.append(measure_rank_margin_and_hit(name, scores, target))\n        return {'metrics': aggregate_paired(records, controls, empty_value=None),\n                'metric_definitions': definitions_with_counts_and_limitations()}\n\n# Match tensor/NumPy conversions and scoring dimensions to the host ModelView.\n# diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis) serializes measured metrics,\n# definitions, missingness, and limitations; it must not invent findings.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines family-specific correctness checks and temporal sensitivity measurements in one diagnostic contract.",
      "negative": "Causality checks, perturbations, and paired ranking comparisons are established techniques."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a reusable basis for deciding which subsequent NextItNet training changes deserve the limited search budget.",
      "negative": "Diagnostic sensitivity is not evidence that changing that sensitivity will improve validation HR@10."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Uses existing inference interfaces and requires no additional fitted model.",
      "negative": "Architecture-specific receptive-field checks and carefully matched history edits require implementation work."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "NextItNet",
      "link": "https://fajieyuan.github.io/papers/nextitnet6.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides the residual dilated causal convolution architecture underlying the required model family.",
        "Supports structural checks of causal dependence and receptive-field eligibility."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Architectural accessibility does not establish how strongly the trained model uses particular interactions.",
        "The supplied baseline implementation must be inspected before applying exact receptive-field bounds."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains why sampled ranking metrics can disagree with full-catalog comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not justify changing the pinned benchmark or treating full-catalog logged-data evaluation as unbiased preference measurement."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Measuring Recency Bias",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09722",
      "contributions": [
        "Motivates measuring recency dependence rather than assuming greater recent-item influence is beneficial."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the optimal temporal dependence for these four categories or the supplied NextItNet checkpoint."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
